Generic single-database configuration.

# SQLite ↔ Postgres portability rules

The demo runs on SQLite (`sqlite+aiosqlite:///./data/cockpit.db`). The
bank-buyer scope (parked, see `docs/architecture.md#Demo Scope Addendum`)
runs on Postgres. Migrations are written to apply cleanly against both —
no Postgres-only features in `versions/*.py`.

When authoring a new migration, prefer:

| Use                          | Avoid                                |
| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `sa.JSON()`                  | `postgresql.JSONB()`                 |
| `sa.String(36)` for UUIDs    | `postgresql.UUID()`                  |
| `sa.String(32)` for ENUMs    | `postgresql.ENUM(...)` / `sa.Enum`   |
| `sa.text("CURRENT_TIMESTAMP")` server defaults | `sa.text("gen_random_uuid()")` and other Postgres-only functions |
| Application-level CHECK via Pydantic + state machine | Native ENUM column types |

`alembic --autogenerate` occasionally infers Postgres-native types
(`UUID`, `JSONB`) when run against a Postgres scratch DB — always
inspect the generated revision file before committing.

# Case state machine

The `cases.state` column stores a free-form `String(32)`; the legal
transitions are enforced in Python by
`contracts.cases.assert_transition` (single source of truth).

```
intake_scheduled ──► decision_ready ──► committed ──► closed
       │                  │                ▲           ▲
       │                  └────► escalated ┘           │
       ├──────────────────────► escalated ─────────────┤
       └──────────────────────► closed ────────────────┘
```

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> intake_scheduled
    intake_scheduled --> decision_ready
    intake_scheduled --> escalated
    intake_scheduled --> closed
    decision_ready --> committed
    decision_ready --> escalated
    decision_ready --> closed
    committed --> closed
    escalated --> committed
    escalated --> closed
    closed --> [*]
```

`closure_date` is populated only on transition into `closed`. `committed`
and `escalated` are non-terminal — a case stays "open" through the 120-second
undo window (Story 7-4) and can return from `escalated` to `committed`.

The diagram above is the canonical reference for Story 7-9 (decision outcomes)
and Story 10-2 (approve-with-conditions). Update both this file and
`packages/contracts/src/contracts/cases.py::ALLOWED_TRANSITIONS` in lock-step.

# Ledger note (Story 3-1)

The demo's ledger is a JSONL file at `./data/ledger.jsonl`, not a Postgres
schema. There are no `ledger_entries` migrations. The bank-buyer scope's
per-tenant `ledger.ledger_entries` table + `BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE`
triggers + `ledger_writer` role would land here if the bank-buyer scope is
revived. For now, append-only is enforced by Python convention — see
`cockpit_api.services.ledger_service`. A unit test asserts the public
surface of `LedgerWriter` is exactly `{"append"}`.
